/**
\page ex_dodge Dodge

<p class="lede">Move left and right to avoid falling blocks. The longer you survive, the faster they come.</p>

<div class="jd-meta"><span><b>Source</b> \exsrc{games/Dodge.cpp}</span><span><b>Executable</b> <code>DodgeGame</code></span><span><b>Controls</b> <kbd>A</kbd> <kbd>D</kbd> or arrow keys to move, <kbd>R</kbd> to restart</span></div>

\image html dodge.png "Dodge: the player (cyan) dodges falling blocks; the HUD is in the top-right corner"

<b>Shows:</b> input actions (\ref input), custom components and systems (\ref ecs), spawning and destroying entities, a cached query for collision, and a HUD made with an editor tool (\ref editor).

## Actions and components

Two input actions: a `float` for left/right, whose bindings add up, and a `bool` for restarting. Three components: a marker for the player, a speed for falling blocks, and a timer for the spawner.

\genexopen{games/Dodge.cpp,Actions and components}
\snippet games/Dodge.cpp actions
\snippet games/Dodge.cpp components
\endex

## Setting up

Textures first, then the engine, then registration, key bindings and the starting entities. The spawner is an entity too: its timer is a component, so a system can update it.

\genexopen{games/Dodge.cpp,main(): setup}
\snippet games/Dodge.cpp setup
\endex

## Systems

The player system moves the player with the `MoveX` action, then checks every falling block for an overlap. It uses a query built once in `main()`:

\genexopen{games/Dodge.cpp,Query and player system}
\snippet games/Dodge.cpp query
\snippet games/Dodge.cpp player_system
\endex

Falling blocks move down and destroy themselves below the screen. `destruct()` inside a system is deferred until the system has finished, so it is safe while iterating:

\genexopen{games/Dodge.cpp,Falling}
\snippet games/Dodge.cpp fall_system
\endex

The spawner counts down and creates a block when it reaches zero. The interval shrinks as the round goes on. After a hit, it waits for <kbd>R</kbd> and clears the board:

\genexopen{games/Dodge.cpp,Spawning and restarting}
\snippet games/Dodge.cpp spawn_system
\endex

## HUD

Game UI is an editor tool. It also collapses the Dear ImGui demo window, which the engine always shows:

\genexopen{games/Dodge.cpp,The HUD}
\snippet games/Dodge.cpp hud
\endex

## Full source

\genex{games/Dodge.cpp,Dodge.cpp}
\include games/Dodge.cpp
\endex
*/

/**
\page ex_snake Snake

<p class="lede">Steer the snake to the red food. Each bite makes it longer and faster; walls and your own tail end the round.</p>

<div class="jd-meta"><span><b>Source</b> \exsrc{games/Snake.cpp}</span><span><b>Executable</b> <code>SnakeGame</code></span><span><b>Controls</b> <kbd>W</kbd> <kbd>A</kbd> <kbd>S</kbd> <kbd>D</kbd> or arrow keys to turn, <kbd>R</kbd> to restart</span></div>

\image html snake.png "Snake: the head is bright green, the food red"

<b>Shows:</b> press bindings with the `OVERRIDE` operator (\ref input), game logic on a fixed time step, keeping game data (grid cells) separate from presentation (transforms), reusing entities instead of destroying them, and changing a sprite's colour at runtime.

## Actions

Four press bindings share one `glm::vec2` action. With `OVERRIDE`, the action's value is the last binding that triggered this frame, so the result is always one of the four directions (or zero):

\genexopen{games/Snake.cpp,Turning with OVERRIDE}
\snippet games/Snake.cpp actions
\snippet games/Snake.cpp bindings
\endex

## Data and presentation

Each body segment has an `FGridCell` (a cell on a 32 × 18 grid). The snake's state is a component on one entity; the order of the body segments is kept in a `std::deque`:

\genexopen{games/Snake.cpp,Components and game state}
\snippet games/Snake.cpp components
\snippet games/Snake.cpp state
\snippet games/Snake.cpp spawn_cell
\endex

A separate system turns grid cells into world positions. It runs in `PostUpdate`, after the game logic:

\genexopen{games/Snake.cpp,Grid to transform}
\snippet games/Snake.cpp snap
\endex

## The step

The snake system reads turns every frame, but only moves when its timer runs out. Moving reuses the tail entity as the new head; growing creates a new head. Components set inside a system are applied after it finishes, which is why `IsOnSnake` uses `try_get`:

\genexopen{games/Snake.cpp,One step of the snake}
\snippet games/Snake.cpp step
\endex

\genexopen{games/Snake.cpp,Starting a round}
\snippet games/Snake.cpp start_round
\endex

## Full source

\genex{games/Snake.cpp,Snake.cpp}
\include games/Snake.cpp
\endex
*/

/**
\page ex_breakout Breakout

<p class="lede">Bounce the ball off your paddle to clear all fifty bricks. You have three balls.</p>

<div class="jd-meta"><span><b>Source</b> \exsrc{games/Breakout.cpp}</span><span><b>Executable</b> <code>BreakoutGame</code></span><span><b>Controls</b> <kbd>A</kbd> <kbd>D</kbd> or arrow keys to move, <kbd>Space</kbd> to launch, <kbd>R</kbd> to restart</span></div>

\image html breakout.png "Breakout: one brick gone, the ball back on the paddle after a miss"

<b>Shows:</b> velocity-based movement, bouncing off walls and rectangles, destroying entities on hit, counting entities with a query to detect a win, and several input actions.

## Components and bricks

\genexopen{games/Breakout.cpp,Actions and components}
\snippet games/Breakout.cpp components
\endex

Bricks are ordinary sprites with an `FBrick` component holding their score:

\genexopen{games/Breakout.cpp,Building the wall of bricks}
\snippet games/Breakout.cpp brick_wall
\snippet games/Breakout.cpp scene
\endex

## Paddle

\genexopen{games/Breakout.cpp,The paddle}
\snippet games/Breakout.cpp paddle
\endex

## Ball

The ball waits on the paddle until <kbd>Space</kbd>:

\genexopen{games/Breakout.cpp,Launching}
\snippet games/Breakout.cpp ball_launch
\endex

It moves by its velocity, bounces off three walls, and is lost below the screen. Hitting the paddle sends it back up at an angle that depends on where it hit:

\genexopen{games/Breakout.cpp,Walls and paddle}
\snippet games/Breakout.cpp ball_walls
\endex

For bricks, the overlap tells which side was hit: a narrow horizontal overlap means a side hit. The brick is destroyed, and when the query counts only the brick being destroyed, the board is clear:

\genexopen{games/Breakout.cpp,Bricks}
\snippet games/Breakout.cpp ball_bricks
\endex

## Full source

\genex{games/Breakout.cpp,Breakout.cpp}
\include games/Breakout.cpp
\endex
*/
